WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.880
The 1918 Nobel Prize for chemistry

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:07.290
is probably the most important
Nobel Prize ever awarded.

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:10.350
It was given to German
scientist Fritz Haber

00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:12.630
for solving one of the biggest problems

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:14.970
humanity has ever faced.

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:17.730
His invention is directly responsible

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:22.170
for the lives of 4 billion people today.

00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:24.540
But when he received his prize,

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:27.990
many of his peers refused to attend,

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:30.870
two other Nobel Prize
winners rejected their awards

00:00:30.870 --> 00:00:33.300
in protest, and "The New York Times" wrote

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:36.270
a scathing article about him.

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:39.930
He is simultaneously one
of the most impactful

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:42.749
and tragic scientists of all time.

00:00:42.749 --> 00:00:46.020
(dramatic music)

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:48.480
Perhaps more than any other single person,

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:51.623
he has shaped the world we live in today.

00:00:51.623 --> 00:00:54.456
(dramatic music)

00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:57.720
Part of this video is sponsored by Wren.

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:00.405
More about them at the end of the show.

00:01:00.405 --> 00:01:03.238
(dramatic music)

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:08.610
If you are an American citizen

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:11.880
and you find an island with
a lot of bird poop on it,

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:15.750
well, then, you can claim that
island for the United States,

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:17.880
and the U.S. will have your back.

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:21.780
The president is authorized to
send in the navy and the army

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:25.890
to defend your newly
discovered poop-covered island.

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:28.230
There are currently 10 American islands

00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:30.960
that were claimed in this way.

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:34.560
And even though the law that
made this possible was passed

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:39.560
in 1856, it is still
in effect to this day.

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:44.851
So why did people want
poop-covered islands so badly?

00:01:44.851 --> 00:01:48.630
(birds chirping)
(cheerful upbeat music)

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:51.540
There are a few dozen
islands off the coast of Peru

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:54.660
where millions of sea birds gather to mate

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:57.840
and the waters near the
island are full of fish,

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:02.703
and these millions of birds eat
the fish and then they poop.

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:04.624
A lot.

00:02:04.624 --> 00:02:06.360
(cheerful upbeat music)

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:10.020
Since the region is hot and
dry, this poop solidifies

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:12.960
and accumulates over millennia.

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:17.960
There are cliffs of bird poop
30 meters, or 100 feet, high.

00:02:18.315 --> 00:02:21.732
(cheerful upbeat music)

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:26.970
Technically bird poop is called guano,

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:30.060
and by the mid 1800s buying and selling

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:32.340
bird guano was big business.

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:36.660
The price rose as high as $76 per pound,

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:39.600
meaning you could trade
four pounds of guano

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:42.090
for one pound of gold.

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:45.690
So why was there such a
big market for bird poop?

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:49.503
Well, to answer that we have
to look inside the human body.

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:55.380
By weight, most of our
bodies are made up of oxygen,

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:57.570
carbon, and hydrogen,

00:02:57.570 --> 00:03:01.530
but the fourth most common
element is nitrogen.

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:05.520
Nitrogen is part of the amino
acids that form proteins.

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:06.840
It's part of hemoglobin,

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:09.600
the compound that carries
oxygen in red blood cells.

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:13.830
And it's a central
component of DNA and RNA.

00:03:13.830 --> 00:03:18.210
Nitrogen is essential
for all life on earth.

00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:21.000
We get our nitrogen by eating plants

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:23.670
or animals which have eaten plants,

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:26.583
and plants get their
nitrogen from the soil.

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:32.070
The problem is if you farm
the same soil year after year

00:03:32.070 --> 00:03:34.830
you harvest the nitrogen out of it,

00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:36.990
and eventually there isn't enough nitrogen

00:03:36.990 --> 00:03:39.090
for healthy plants to grow.

00:03:39.090 --> 00:03:42.390
They can't produce enough
chlorophyll to photosynthesize,

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:44.760
which stunts their growth.

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:46.200
Their leaves turn yellow

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:49.590
and they are more susceptible
to pests and disease.

00:03:49.590 --> 00:03:51.240
Crucially for farmers,

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:55.410
nitrogen deficiency means smaller yields.

00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:59.700
The way to fix this is to add
nitrogen back into the soil,

00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:01.953
which is where bird guano comes in.

00:04:02.808 --> 00:04:03.810
(lively music)

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:07.260
Guano is up to 20% nitrogen.

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:10.140
Hundreds of years ago,
Incan farmers realized

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:13.710
that adding guano to their
soil made crops grow taller.

00:04:13.710 --> 00:04:15.750
This is what allowed them to grow food

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:18.210
in places that were previously un-farmable

00:04:18.210 --> 00:04:20.301
and expand their empire.

00:04:20.301 --> 00:04:21.960
(lively music)

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:24.330
South America's rich deposits of bird poop

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:27.150
did not go unnoticed by
the rest of the world.

00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:31.050
In 1865, Spain went to war
against its former colonies

00:04:31.050 --> 00:04:33.840
of Peru, Chile, Ecuador, and Bolivia

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:37.800
for control of their guano-laden islands.

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:39.990
But such was the world's
appetite for nitrogen

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:43.890
that by 1872 guano was running out

00:04:43.890 --> 00:04:46.470
and Peru banned further exports.

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:50.780
The world would need another
way to get its nitrogen fix.

00:04:50.780 --> 00:04:53.250
(dramatic music)

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:55.230
This was a crisis.

00:04:55.230 --> 00:04:57.120
William Crookes, a British chemist,

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:00.240
made a dire prophecy in 1898.

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:02.100
With the world's growing population

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:05.317
and dwindling supplies
of nitrogen, he said,

00:05:05.317 --> 00:05:09.360
"We stand in deadly peril of
not having enough to eat."

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:11.940
In less than 30 years time, he argued,

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:16.500
people all over the world
will be dying of starvation.

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:19.687
But he also proposed a solution.

00:05:19.687 --> 00:05:22.957
"It is the chemist who
must come to the rescue.

00:05:22.957 --> 00:05:24.457
"It is through the laboratory

00:05:24.457 --> 00:05:28.220
"that starvation may ultimately
be turned into plenty."

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:30.330
(dramatic music)

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:32.070
Because here's the thing,

00:05:32.070 --> 00:05:35.250
nitrogen isn't rare, it's common.

00:05:35.250 --> 00:05:39.120
78% of the air is nitrogen,

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:42.720
but it's in a form that
plants and animals can't use:

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:47.550
two atoms of nitrogen
triple bonded together.

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:51.360
This bond is one of the
strongest in nature.

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:53.640
The way to measure the
strength of a chemical bond

00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:57.060
is by the amount of energy
that's required to break it.

00:05:57.060 --> 00:06:00.060
So to break apart two
chlorine atoms, for example,

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:03.300
would take two and a half electron volts.

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:06.420
To break apart two
carbons requires 3.8 eV.

00:06:07.703 --> 00:06:10.800
Two oxygens, 5.2 eV.

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:13.620
But to break apart two atoms of nitrogen

00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:18.620
requires 9.8 electron volts,
a tremendous amount of energy.

00:06:19.894 --> 00:06:23.453
(atoms cracking)

00:06:23.453 --> 00:06:25.020
(thunder cracking)

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:27.813
There are two processes
that do this naturally.

00:06:29.490 --> 00:06:32.220
Lightning releases so much energy

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:36.750
it breaks apart N2 into
individual nitrogen atoms.

00:06:36.750 --> 00:06:40.890
They then quickly react
to form nitrogen oxides,

00:06:40.890 --> 00:06:43.740
and these molecules stay in the atmosphere

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:47.040
until they react with
water droplets in clouds

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:49.950
and fall to the ground in rain.

00:06:49.950 --> 00:06:53.130
There are also a few types
of bacteria living in soil

00:06:53.130 --> 00:06:55.290
that can break the N2 bond,

00:06:55.290 --> 00:06:58.497
using a tremendous amount
of energy to do so,

00:06:58.497 --> 00:07:02.370
and they make nitrogen
available for plants.

00:07:02.370 --> 00:07:05.850
But bacteria only replenished
the nitrogen slowly,

00:07:05.850 --> 00:07:07.410
and there's not enough lightning

00:07:07.410 --> 00:07:12.390
to produce nitrogen compounds
at scale, so chemists tried.

00:07:12.390 --> 00:07:16.620
In 1811, Georg Hildebrandt
mixed nitrogen and hydrogen

00:07:16.620 --> 00:07:19.950
in a sealed flask, trying to make ammonia,

00:07:19.950 --> 00:07:23.613
one of the nitrogen-containing
molecules found in guano.

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:27.390
When that didn't work,
he submerged the flask

00:07:27.390 --> 00:07:31.290
300 meters underwater to
increase the pressure,

00:07:31.290 --> 00:07:35.250
and that didn't work either,
but he was on the right track.

00:07:35.250 --> 00:07:37.290
Increasingly sophisticated versions

00:07:37.290 --> 00:07:38.940
of these experiments were carried out

00:07:38.940 --> 00:07:41.160
over the following 100 years.

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:42.843
All of them failed.

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:47.040
So when Fritz Haber became
interested in this problem

00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:52.040
in 1904, he was joining a
long line of failed chemists.

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:55.950
He was 36 years old, working
as a low level academic

00:07:55.950 --> 00:07:58.350
at the University of Karlsruhe.

00:07:58.350 --> 00:08:00.060
He was also a new father

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:03.720
with a two-year-old boy named
Hermann and a wife, Clara,

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:08.010
who was one of the first women
to get a PhD in chemistry.

00:08:08.010 --> 00:08:11.340
Driven by pride and competition
with another scientist,

00:08:11.340 --> 00:08:14.340
Haber spent five years on the problem.

00:08:14.340 --> 00:08:17.520
His idea was to combine
nitrogen and hydrogen

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:21.660
not only at high pressure,
but also at high temperature

00:08:21.660 --> 00:08:23.580
and in the presence of a catalyst,

00:08:23.580 --> 00:08:26.310
something that lowers the
amount of energy required

00:08:26.310 --> 00:08:28.980
to split diatomic nitrogen.

00:08:28.980 --> 00:08:33.090
To do this, new experimental
apparatus had to be invented.

00:08:33.090 --> 00:08:35.850
Haber worked tirelessly on this project,

00:08:35.850 --> 00:08:37.560
building equipment that could tolerate

00:08:37.560 --> 00:08:40.530
ever higher temperatures and pressures.

00:08:40.530 --> 00:08:42.420
He also got lucky.

00:08:42.420 --> 00:08:45.450
At the time he was moonlighting
as a technical consultant

00:08:45.450 --> 00:08:47.790
for a light bulb manufacturer.

00:08:47.790 --> 00:08:49.530
So there he had access

00:08:49.530 --> 00:08:52.770
to lots of really hard-to-find materials,

00:08:52.770 --> 00:08:54.870
like the element osmium.

00:08:54.870 --> 00:08:56.550
Osmium is rare.

00:08:56.550 --> 00:08:59.610
In his day, there was
only about 100 kilograms

00:08:59.610 --> 00:09:02.100
of the refined metal in existence.

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:04.500
But the company he worked
for was experimenting

00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:07.260
with using it for filaments
in their light bulbs,

00:09:07.260 --> 00:09:10.800
so they had most of the world's supply.

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:14.250
Haber suspected it might
make the perfect catalyst,

00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:16.893
so he brought a sample back to his lab.

00:09:17.790 --> 00:09:20.850
And there in the third week of March 1909,

00:09:20.850 --> 00:09:24.900
Haber placed his sheet of
osmium in the pressure chamber,

00:09:24.900 --> 00:09:28.170
and then he pressurized and
heated the nitrogen and hydrogen

00:09:28.170 --> 00:09:33.000
to 200 atmospheres and
500 degrees Celsius.

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:37.380
Under these conditions the
triple bonds broke apart

00:09:37.380 --> 00:09:40.470
and nitrogen reacted with hydrogen.

00:09:40.470 --> 00:09:45.470
Of the total gas mixture,
6% turned into ammonia.

00:09:45.900 --> 00:09:47.550
When the gas was cooled,

00:09:47.550 --> 00:09:50.280
one milliliter of ammonia
dripped out the end

00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:52.293
of a narrow tube into a beaker.

00:09:53.190 --> 00:09:56.617
An elated Haber rushed from
one lab to another yelling,

00:09:56.617 --> 00:09:59.307
"Come on down! There's ammonia!"

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:03.630
Germany's biggest chemical company, BASF,

00:10:03.630 --> 00:10:05.760
commercialized Haber's process.

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:08.880
Within four years they had
opened a factory in Oppau,

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:12.535
producing five tons of ammonia per day.

00:10:12.535 --> 00:10:13.841
(lively music)

00:10:13.841 --> 00:10:15.360
(singers singing in a foreign language)

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:18.366
People spoke of making bread from the air.

00:10:18.366 --> 00:10:21.480
(singers singing in a foreign language)

00:10:21.480 --> 00:10:24.270
With the fertilizer from
this industrial process

00:10:24.270 --> 00:10:25.980
on the same plot of land,

00:10:25.980 --> 00:10:29.610
farmers were able to grow
four times as much food,

00:10:29.610 --> 00:10:34.260
and as a result the population
of the Earth quadrupled.

00:10:34.260 --> 00:10:36.810
There's a good chance you owe your life

00:10:36.810 --> 00:10:38.640
to Haber's invention.

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:42.030
The Earth supports 4
billion more people today

00:10:42.030 --> 00:10:44.793
than it could without nitrogen fertilizer.

00:10:45.900 --> 00:10:49.440
In fact, around 50% of the nitrogen atoms

00:10:49.440 --> 00:10:53.013
in your body came from the Haber process.

00:10:55.980 --> 00:10:59.400
The invention made Fritz
Haber a wealthy man.

00:10:59.400 --> 00:11:02.400
He got a promotion, becoming
the founding director

00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:04.080
of the Kaiser Wilhelm Institute

00:11:04.080 --> 00:11:06.960
for Physical Chemistry in Berlin.

00:11:06.960 --> 00:11:10.230
He also befriended some of the
best scientists of his day,

00:11:10.230 --> 00:11:14.880
including Max Planck, Max
Born, and Albert Einstein.

00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:18.690
After Einstein separated
from his first wife in 1914,

00:11:18.690 --> 00:11:21.123
he stayed the night at Haber's house.

00:11:21.960 --> 00:11:26.460
But if Haber was so well
regarded, why was he shunned

00:11:26.460 --> 00:11:29.193
by colleagues when he won the Nobel Prize?

00:11:30.120 --> 00:11:33.453
Well, it all comes down to
what happened in World War I.

00:11:35.820 --> 00:11:40.470
When the war broke out, Haber
volunteered for military duty.

00:11:40.470 --> 00:11:43.560
Unlike pacifist Einstein
who denounced the war,

00:11:43.560 --> 00:11:45.420
Haber was a patriot.

00:11:45.420 --> 00:11:49.650
He wanted to use his
expertise to help his country.

00:11:49.650 --> 00:11:51.690
Only a few months into the war,

00:11:51.690 --> 00:11:54.030
the German army was already running out

00:11:54.030 --> 00:11:56.430
of gun powder and explosives.

00:11:56.430 --> 00:11:59.880
Ammonium nitrate, besides
being an excellent fertilizer,

00:11:59.880 --> 00:12:02.160
is also an explosive.

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:05.807
Just look at what happened
in Beirut in August of 2020.

00:12:05.807 --> 00:12:08.974
(warehouse exploding)

00:12:11.850 --> 00:12:15.270
A warehouse containing almost 3,000 tons

00:12:15.270 --> 00:12:17.910
of ammonium nitrate caught fire,

00:12:17.910 --> 00:12:22.320
and in the extreme heat
the fertilizer detonated.

00:12:22.320 --> 00:12:26.280
The blast, which could be heard
hundreds of kilometers away,

00:12:26.280 --> 00:12:31.280
killed at least 217 people
and injured thousands more.

00:12:31.380 --> 00:12:34.440
Seismometers registered
an artificial earthquake

00:12:34.440 --> 00:12:37.440
measuring 3.3 on the Richter scale.

00:12:37.440 --> 00:12:41.850
This is just one of many
fertilizer-related explosions.

00:12:41.850 --> 00:12:44.430
The Oppau plant where Haber's process

00:12:44.430 --> 00:12:48.753
was first put into practice
would also explode in 1921.

00:12:50.130 --> 00:12:52.560
And the reason is nitrogen.

00:12:52.560 --> 00:12:54.330
We've already seen that it takes

00:12:54.330 --> 00:12:56.100
a tremendous amount of energy

00:12:56.100 --> 00:12:59.280
to break apart nitrogen's triple bond.

00:12:59.280 --> 00:13:01.050
But the flip side of that coin

00:13:01.050 --> 00:13:03.180
is that when two nitrogen
atoms come together

00:13:03.180 --> 00:13:04.474
and form that bond,

00:13:04.474 --> 00:13:05.370
(N2 model clicking)

00:13:05.370 --> 00:13:09.240
a huge amount of energy is released.

00:13:09.240 --> 00:13:12.810
The explosions of gun
powder, TNT, nitroglycerin,

00:13:12.810 --> 00:13:17.280
and ammonium nitrate all
form diatomic nitrogen gas

00:13:17.280 --> 00:13:20.880
as a product, and the
formation of that triple bond

00:13:20.880 --> 00:13:23.010
is where these chemicals derive

00:13:23.010 --> 00:13:25.353
much of their explosive energy.

00:13:26.880 --> 00:13:30.180
Haber lobbied to convert the
factories using his process

00:13:30.180 --> 00:13:32.010
to make ammonia for fertilizer

00:13:32.010 --> 00:13:35.253
to create nitrate for explosives instead.

00:13:36.390 --> 00:13:39.990
His superiors believed such a
conversion to be impossible,

00:13:39.990 --> 00:13:41.490
but Haber persisted,

00:13:41.490 --> 00:13:44.970
and soon his chemical
process was at the heart

00:13:44.970 --> 00:13:47.160
of the German war machine.

00:13:47.160 --> 00:13:51.993
From bread out of the air
to bombs out of the air.

00:13:56.280 --> 00:13:58.140
But Haber thought chemistry could make

00:13:58.140 --> 00:14:00.840
an even bigger contribution to the war.

00:14:00.840 --> 00:14:04.920
In December 1914, he witnessed
a chemical weapons test.

00:14:04.920 --> 00:14:07.440
He was unimpressed.

00:14:07.440 --> 00:14:10.200
Haber believed that he could do better.

00:14:10.200 --> 00:14:12.690
He set out to make a gas that was deadly

00:14:12.690 --> 00:14:16.470
at low concentrations and heavier than air

00:14:16.470 --> 00:14:19.920
so it would sink into enemy trenches.

00:14:19.920 --> 00:14:22.650
Projectiles carrying
chemical weapons were banned,

00:14:22.650 --> 00:14:27.060
at least in theory, by the
Hague Convention of 1899,

00:14:27.060 --> 00:14:29.310
but in practice after
the start of the war,

00:14:29.310 --> 00:14:32.280
Germany, France, and
Britain all experimented

00:14:32.280 --> 00:14:34.410
with chemical weapons.

00:14:34.410 --> 00:14:36.840
Haber converted his wing of the institute

00:14:36.840 --> 00:14:39.240
into a chemical weapons laboratory,

00:14:39.240 --> 00:14:41.400
and after only a few months of work

00:14:41.400 --> 00:14:44.760
he zeroed in on chlorine gas.

00:14:44.760 --> 00:14:47.580
An employee, Otto Hahn,
expressed his discomfort

00:14:47.580 --> 00:14:49.140
about the new weapon.

00:14:49.140 --> 00:14:52.837
Haber told him, "Innumerable
human lives would be saved

00:14:52.837 --> 00:14:55.797
"if the war could be ended
more quickly in this way."

00:15:00.090 --> 00:15:02.670
At 6 p.m. on the 22nd of April,

00:15:02.670 --> 00:15:05.520
with the wind blowing
toward the Allied trenches,

00:15:05.520 --> 00:15:09.060
German troops released
168 tons of chlorine

00:15:09.060 --> 00:15:12.273
from over 5,000 gas cylinders.

00:15:13.350 --> 00:15:17.340
The wall of gas advanced
across the battlefield.

00:15:17.340 --> 00:15:21.090
Since chlorine gas is two and
a half times heavier than air,

00:15:21.090 --> 00:15:24.393
it sank into the trenches
of the Allied soldiers.

00:15:25.350 --> 00:15:28.680
Any soldier that inhaled
a lung full of the gas

00:15:28.680 --> 00:15:31.230
suffered a terrible death.

00:15:31.230 --> 00:15:34.380
Chlorine irritates the
mucus lining of the lungs

00:15:34.380 --> 00:15:37.413
so violently that they fill with liquid.

00:15:38.670 --> 00:15:42.803
The soldiers effectively
drowned on dry land.

00:15:42.803 --> 00:15:45.030
(solemn music)

00:15:45.030 --> 00:15:48.240
More than 5,000 Allied
soldiers died this way

00:15:48.240 --> 00:15:49.357
in the first attack.

00:15:49.357 --> 00:15:52.024
(solemn music)

00:15:54.090 --> 00:15:57.690
Haber was promoted to the rank of captain,

00:15:57.690 --> 00:16:00.003
and a week later he was
back home in Berlin.

00:16:01.260 --> 00:16:04.980
On the 1st of May, the
Habers hosted a dinner party,

00:16:04.980 --> 00:16:07.050
and after the party wound down,

00:16:07.050 --> 00:16:09.723
Fritz took sleeping pills and went to bed.

00:16:11.010 --> 00:16:14.220
But that night his wife Clara took his gun

00:16:14.220 --> 00:16:16.740
and went outside into the garden,

00:16:16.740 --> 00:16:20.879
and there she fired a
single shot into her chest.

00:16:20.879 --> 00:16:23.556
(gunshot)

00:16:23.556 --> 00:16:24.450
(melancholy music)

00:16:24.450 --> 00:16:27.060
Her 12-year-old son,
Hermann, heard the shot

00:16:27.060 --> 00:16:31.038
and ran outside to find his
mother as she lay dying.

00:16:31.038 --> 00:16:33.955
(melancholy music)

00:16:35.040 --> 00:16:37.500
The next morning, Fritz
Haber was on a train

00:16:37.500 --> 00:16:41.880
to the eastern front to
supervise a gas attack

00:16:41.880 --> 00:16:43.438
on the Russian army.

00:16:43.438 --> 00:16:44.760
(melancholy music)

00:16:44.760 --> 00:16:47.220
Some have claimed Clara killed herself

00:16:47.220 --> 00:16:50.670
because of her husband's
obsession with chemical weapons.

00:16:50.670 --> 00:16:52.710
And that may have been part of it,

00:16:52.710 --> 00:16:54.810
but honestly we don't know

00:16:54.810 --> 00:16:58.680
because no firsthand accounts survive

00:16:58.680 --> 00:17:00.960
that support this interpretation.

00:17:00.960 --> 00:17:04.830
What we do know is that
Clara was deeply unhappy

00:17:04.830 --> 00:17:06.240
in her marriage.

00:17:06.240 --> 00:17:10.410
In 1910, after being married
for eight years to Fritz,

00:17:10.410 --> 00:17:12.427
she wrote to a friend,

00:17:12.427 --> 00:17:15.037
"What Fritz has won
during these eight years,

00:17:15.037 --> 00:17:18.067
"that, and still more, I have lost.

00:17:18.067 --> 00:17:20.257
"And what remains ahead of me fills me

00:17:20.257 --> 00:17:24.480
"with the deepest dissatisfaction."

00:17:24.480 --> 00:17:27.960
After Clara's suicide, Haber
spent the rest of the war

00:17:27.960 --> 00:17:30.720
running his institute,
researching chemical weapons,

00:17:30.720 --> 00:17:33.030
gas masks, and pesticides.

00:17:33.030 --> 00:17:36.570
By 1917, the institute
employed 1,500 people,

00:17:36.570 --> 00:17:38.940
including 150 scientists.

00:17:38.940 --> 00:17:41.430
It was like a mini Manhattan Project,

00:17:41.430 --> 00:17:43.053
but for chemical weapons.

00:17:44.070 --> 00:17:47.400
In total, 100,000 soldiers were killed

00:17:47.400 --> 00:17:49.150
by chemical weapons in World War I.

00:17:50.250 --> 00:17:53.700
When Germany surrendered,
Haber was crushed.

00:17:53.700 --> 00:17:56.310
All the money he made
from his ammonia patent

00:17:56.310 --> 00:17:58.710
was lost to hyperinflation.

00:17:58.710 --> 00:18:02.100
In an attempt to pay off
Germany's crippling war debt,

00:18:02.100 --> 00:18:05.760
he tried to distill gold from seawater,

00:18:05.760 --> 00:18:07.563
but the project was futile.

00:18:08.940 --> 00:18:12.300
In 1933, the Nazis came
to power and passed a law

00:18:12.300 --> 00:18:15.420
that all Jewish civil
servants, including scientists,

00:18:15.420 --> 00:18:17.643
were to be fired from their jobs.

00:18:18.780 --> 00:18:22.650
Haber was Jewish, but he
never practiced the religion.

00:18:22.650 --> 00:18:25.950
Regardless, his military service
exempted him from the law,

00:18:25.950 --> 00:18:28.980
but he resigned from his role
as director in solidarity

00:18:28.980 --> 00:18:32.339
with all the Jewish scientists
who worked at the institute.

00:18:32.339 --> 00:18:34.800
(melancholy music)

00:18:34.800 --> 00:18:38.550
The next year, in a hotel
room in Basel, Switzerland,

00:18:38.550 --> 00:18:40.623
he died of heart failure.

00:18:49.710 --> 00:18:51.180
Immediately after World War I,

00:18:51.180 --> 00:18:55.890
Haber's institute developed
a cyanide-based insecticide.

00:18:55.890 --> 00:18:58.530
It had a barely detectable odor,

00:18:58.530 --> 00:19:01.320
so they mixed in a foul-smelling chemical

00:19:01.320 --> 00:19:03.930
to alert people to the danger.

00:19:03.930 --> 00:19:07.023
The resulting gas was called Zyklon B.

00:19:07.860 --> 00:19:11.340
A decade after Haber's
death, the Nazis requested

00:19:11.340 --> 00:19:14.820
chemists remove the
foul-smelling component,

00:19:14.820 --> 00:19:17.280
and this form of Zyklon B,

00:19:17.280 --> 00:19:20.400
the chemical developed
at Haber's institute,

00:19:20.400 --> 00:19:23.590
was then used to perpetrate the Holocaust.

00:19:23.590 --> 00:19:26.257
(solemn music)

00:19:29.820 --> 00:19:31.890
Thinking about this story,

00:19:31.890 --> 00:19:35.310
it would be easy to
paint Haber as a villain

00:19:35.310 --> 00:19:38.880
or as a hero for inventing the process

00:19:38.880 --> 00:19:41.103
used to feed half the world.

00:19:42.180 --> 00:19:46.200
But another approach is to
regard him as irrelevant

00:19:46.200 --> 00:19:48.210
to the larger story

00:19:48.210 --> 00:19:50.910
because someone else
would've figured out a way

00:19:50.910 --> 00:19:53.700
to process nitrogen out of the air,

00:19:53.700 --> 00:19:57.213
and other scientists were
developing chemical weapons.

00:19:58.230 --> 00:20:00.060
Over the past few centuries

00:20:00.060 --> 00:20:05.040
science and technology have
improved our lives immeasurably,

00:20:05.040 --> 00:20:08.640
but they have also given
us ever increasing ways

00:20:08.640 --> 00:20:11.040
to destroy ourselves.

00:20:11.040 --> 00:20:12.300
I think it'd be great to believe

00:20:12.300 --> 00:20:16.410
that we could ask scientists
to only work on problems

00:20:16.410 --> 00:20:18.450
that are good for humanity,

00:20:18.450 --> 00:20:21.420
but the reality is that
every bit of information

00:20:21.420 --> 00:20:24.060
is a potential double-edged sword.

00:20:24.060 --> 00:20:26.640
You don't know the
outcome of your research

00:20:26.640 --> 00:20:29.670
or how it might later be used.

00:20:29.670 --> 00:20:34.653
Ammonium nitrate is both a
fertilizer and an explosive.

00:20:35.580 --> 00:20:39.900
So the real question is
how do we keep increasing

00:20:39.900 --> 00:20:43.980
our knowledge and control
of the natural world

00:20:43.980 --> 00:20:45.660
without destroying ourselves

00:20:45.660 --> 00:20:48.753
and everything else on
this planet in the process?

00:20:55.260 --> 00:20:57.330
So chemistry has made it possible

00:20:57.330 --> 00:20:59.910
for 8 billion of us to live on this planet

00:20:59.910 --> 00:21:02.640
and to have the standard
of living that we do,

00:21:02.640 --> 00:21:05.400
but as a byproduct, we've
changed the atmosphere

00:21:05.400 --> 00:21:06.930
and now we're suffering the consequences

00:21:06.930 --> 00:21:09.540
in the form of more frequent
and severe heat waves,

00:21:09.540 --> 00:21:10.890
among other things.

00:21:10.890 --> 00:21:13.920
Which brings me to an offer
directly from me to you.

